    <item>
      <title>Data Rectangling with jq</title>
      <link>/2017/12/11/data-rectangling-with-jq/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 11 Dec 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/2017/12/11/data-rectangling-with-jq/</guid>
      <description>&lt;script src=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/htmlwidgets/htmlwidgets.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&lt;script src=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/jquery/jquery.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&lt;link href=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/datatables-css/datatables-crosstalk.css&#34; rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; /&gt;&#xA;&lt;script src=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/datatables-binding/datatables.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&lt;link href=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/dt-core/css/jquery.dataTables.min.css&#34; rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; /&gt;&#xA;&lt;link href=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/dt-core/css/jquery.dataTables.extra.css&#34; rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; /&gt;&#xA;&lt;script src=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/dt-core/js/jquery.dataTables.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&lt;link href=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/crosstalk/css/crosstalk.css&#34; rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; /&gt;&#xA;&lt;script src=&#34;../rmarkdown-libs/crosstalk/js/crosstalk.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;“Data rectangling”: the process of turning highly nested data structures (e.g. JSON, XML) into a tabular format.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Data rectangling is a brilliant turn of phrase coined by Jenny Bryan (UBC, RStudio) and leader in the #rstats community. &lt;a href=&#34;https://speakerdeck.com/jennybc/data-rectangling&#34;&gt;Recording&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href=&#34;https://speakerdeck.com/jennybc/data-rectangling&#34;&gt;slides&lt;/a&gt; of Jenny’s talk on the subject give a much better intro to the idea and working with this in R, particularly through the &lt;code&gt;purrr&lt;/code&gt; package.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As nice as &lt;code&gt;purrr&lt;/code&gt; is for the task, I’ve recently found that the &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/ropensci/jqr&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;jqr&lt;/code&gt; package&lt;/a&gt; from &lt;a href=&#34;https://scottchamberlain.info/&#34;&gt;Scott Chamberlain&lt;/a&gt; and co can be a much easier way to go about rectangling your JSON. Here’s a quick comparison based on an example from the &lt;a href=&#34;https://dcl-2017-04.github.io/curriculum/rectangling.html&#34;&gt;lesson&lt;/a&gt; Hadley Jenny have on Data Rectangling.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
